  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA lead shortwave in a broad upper trough will cross the eastern\nGreat Lakes and Upper Ohio Valley region this morning. Isolated\nshowers, and possibly a thunderstorm, are expected with this \ntrough mainly to the north of PIT where more favorable moisture\nwas located. This shortwave is expected to be the focus for a more\nfavorable severe weather potential east of the area this\nafternoon.\n\nIsolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms are expected to\nredevelop this afternoon across the area with the approach and\npassage of a cold front. A lack of deep layer moisture will \nlikely limit thunderstorm coverage, with dry air entrainment \nalso limiting updraft strength. Warming in the mid levels could \nalso limit thunderstorm development, and the severe weather \npotential this afternoon. The dry air aloft could enhance \ndowndrafts for any storms that are able to become more organized\nthrough the afternoon and early evening. Ensembles indicate ML \nCAPE mainly between 1000 and 1500 j/kg this afternoon, with \n0-6km shear around 20kt. The Storm Prediction Center has \nmaintained a Marginal Risk for severe storms across portions of \nthe area, mainly east of a FKL-PIT-CDI line. \n\nConvection should quickly wane this evening with the loss of\ninstability, and the passage of the cold front. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe cold front is expected to exit the region tonight, with\npartial clearing in the evening. Areas of fog and stratus are \nthen expected to develop overnight into early Friday morning,\nespecially where rain occurs this afternoon. \n\nSurface high pressure is expected to build under broad troughing\naloft on Friday, with dry weather expected. This trend should\ncontinue into the weekend, as the surface high tracks east\nacross the region. Rising 500mb heights should also result in a\ngradual warming trend over the weekend as well. \n\nAn upper high is progged to develop across the Southeast CONUS\nby early next week. Models differ on the strength of this\nhigh/ridge, and its northern extent. Current indications are a\nseries of shortwaves are expected to track along the northern\nperiphery of the high, which should return low chances for\nshowers and thunderstorms to portions of the area Monday and\nTuesday. Increasing 500mb heights should also result in above\naverage temperatures into the middle of next week. Ensembles \nindicate the ridge should flatten toward mid week, with \nincreasing showers and storm chances.",
